aised and $2.2 million on hand — with the DCCC already on air, while Jennifer Balkcom's fundraising is not yet reported", "The open seat's Republican lean is offset by a strongly favorable national environment for Democrats this year", "Prediction markets price Ager around a 69 percent win chance, above the mostly Tossup expert consensus", "Polling is thin: a single D-sponsored September poll shows Ager up three, and one rater still calls the race Lean R"], "rationale": "An open Republican-leaning seat collides with a Democratic-favorable environment and a lopsided fundraising gap: Ager has raised over $3 million, the DCCC is advertising, and markets put his win odds near 69 percent despite a Tossup expert consensus. The lone independent-ish datapoint is a partisan September poll showing Ager up three, so a modest Democratic edge with wide uncertainty is the best-supported read.", "watch": "If nonpartisan polling emerges showing Balkcom closer than the markets imply, or if Republican outside groups consolidate spending behind her, this moves back toward a pure tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Open seat: Chuck Edwards (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Western North Carolina's 11th District is an open-seat battleground after Republican Chuck Edwards decided not to run again. The district carries a modest Republican lean, but both Jamie Ager and Jennifer Balkcom are campaigning on local mountain roots, and the national political environment this year tilts toward Democrats, pulling the seat toward the middle.\n\nThe evidence points to a narrow Democratic edge. Ager has raised about $3.05 million and holds $2.2 million on hand, the DCCC has launched its first television ad, and a September poll showed him ahead by three points. Markets on Kalshi and Polymarket give him roughly a 69 percent chance of winning. Most raters still call the race a Tossup, however, and one rates it Lean Republican, reflecting how little independent polling exists. The fore